{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHLUZQXcaxSfuYViKGXA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've updated both warmups files for day 22. Day 22 was zlib (header `78da`) wrapped in standard padded base64; Mastery line 22 is 20-char fragments, each its own zlib stream. The day-21 solver handled it with no changes to its decoding: Single was CORRECT in about 12 s, Time Trial in 2.4 s, and Mastery (108 sets × 22 lines) on the first try. Mastery took 15.9 s to solve, against 0.34 s on day 21, so it's worth watching against the 60 s limit.\n\n1. **`reference/techniques/warmups.md`** (212 lines): I added the day-22 entry, with the one-line `zlib.decompress(base64...)` command and the note that `gzip -dc` won't read a zlib header. I also recorded a pattern: since day 19, one day brings a new kind of encoding and the next day changes one setting of it (day 19→20, day 21→22). I merged days 21–22 into one \"compressor under a text wrapper\" variant axis and squeezed the older day entries to keep the file short. I also added a pitfall: the solver only recognises zlib when the first byte is `0x78`. Other valid zlib headers and headerless deflate would fall through to garbage; the fix is the standard `(CMF<<8|FLG)%31==0` header check.\n2. **`reference/coverage/warmups.md`**: the zlib row is now marked `seen D22`. The row for the general compression sweep (now in the gap list as \"seen\") is gone from the gaps, and the compression rows built last night are relabelled as prebuilt. I added rows 146–156, and the recognition cues now cover `eN` zlib and the \"Another …\" callback, at exactly 60 lines. The JSON gap list is valid with the same keys.\n3. **Gap queue**: it now holds 12 prebuild gaps not yet built. Priority 1 is a zlib header sweep (likely next tweak), Flask/itsdangerous cookies, SAML redirect deflate and JWT/JWE tokens. Behind them are PDF filter chains, XOR keyed off known compression magic, OpenSSL `S